title the git nib carry met an already-pushed commit, and took the follow-up shape
prompt Continue the incense overnight loop per the baton and recursion-prompts/incense-inner.md (same lap as 20261001.074025).
think after committing and pushing the index-row repair (e54225be6) to xy, ran tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish write amend as rule 2 asks for a work commit -- it wrote the parent hash b52d350dff into construction/ITINERARY.md, which is the correct VALUE for an amend but the WRONG shape, since e54225be6 was already pushed and amending a pushed commit asks for a force-push this tree forbids.
think caught this against the lesson the prior lap (20261001.054528) recorded in its own file field: check whether the commit being carried forward has already been pushed before choosing amend over follow-up.
think reverted the stray amend-shape edit with git checkout, ran tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish write follow-up instead, which wrote HEAD (e54225be63) -- read before this follow-up commit -- and committed it as its own commit 586e62a, pushed clean to both xy and debrided.
think ran remember_git_nib_witness.rish GREEN afterward: card nib resolves to state parent.
think this log itself is a second follow-up in the same lap -- the inner.md state update below is a third commit, so the nib was written a second time via write follow-up, reading HEAD (586e62af26) before this commit, per remember-git-nib.md rule 5 (any commit landing beside the work commit carries the nib forward).
obs the amend-vs-follow-up choice cannot be inferred from repository state alone, only from whether a push already happened -- the tool prints both candidates when asked bare, and a hand choosing wrong writes a value that resolves and sits one state past what the witness accepts, reddening on the next lap rather than this one.
file construction/ITINERARY.md -- Git nib field carried from e54225be63 to 586e62af26, follow-up shape both times
file recursion-prompts/incense-inner.md -- lap 20261001.074025's account extended with the nib-carry lesson and the push/commit hashes
